    {
      "blockId": "tour:contentBlock_text:987c9d945e59a4df",
      "field": "contentBlock.text",
      "sourceTextSha256": "62b958b6754ae8416b5b405ee91d4cd5fd5db7a65ab75ecfc4df04ed8b9399f6",
      "provenance": {
        "kind": "ai_translation",
        "runId": "ada-en-work-20261107-d7e179628a0b",
        "model": "ChatGPT"
      },
      "text": "Join a group tour of the Brazilian side of Iguazu Falls with an English-speaking guide.\nIguazu Falls lie in the border region of Brazil, Argentina and Paraguay. Here, 275 cascades plunge from heights of 60 to 80 metres. The falls are in a national park with distinctive flora and fauna. They formed through volcanic activity and shifts in the Earth's crust. In Guarani, Iguazu means ‘big water’. The individual falls have their own names, and the water thunders into the gorge known as the Devil’s Throat. Upstream, the Iguazu River flows gently, in striking contrast to the churning water plunging 72 metres into the gorge. You could spend hours watching it and reflecting on the scale and timelessness of the universe."
    },
    {
      "blockId": "tour:contentBlock_text:f6f0c17232a884f6",
      "field": "contentBlock.text",
      "sourceTextSha256": "b9f6a24a4583f089a66edfc4685e7327f30001558c07035468d1fbfdcfdf0e4e",
      "provenance": {
        "kind": "ai_translation",
        "runId": "ada-en-work-20261107-d7e179628a0b",
        "model": "ChatGPT"
      },
      "text": "You can add a visit to the Bird Park (USD 50 per person), near Iguaçu National Park. Step into large aviaries where brightly coloured birds fly and walk freely among the rainforest trees."
    },
    {
      "blockId": "tour:contentBlock_text:4b4eef9a8c9976d1",
      "field": "contentBlock.text",
      "sourceTextSha256": "173a1bbd652b36924ab1be0007a626f1923e98abfcca63ce0cc12c76e87982f8",
      "provenance": {
        "kind": "ai_translation",
        "runId": "ada-en-work-20261107-d7e179628a0b",
        "model": "ChatGPT"
      },
      "text": "You can add the Macuco Safari (USD 130 per person). Ride about 3 kilometres through the forest in the open back of a jeep while your guide explains the region’s ecology. Then walk to the bank of the Iguazu River and take a motorboat close to the falls. Expect spray, rainbows and plenty of chances to take photos."
    }
  ]
